        "id": 589,
        "title": "N-ary Tree Preorder Traversal",
        "category": "Trees & Graphs",
        "difficulty": "Easy",
        "description": "<div style=\"background: rgba(46, 213, 115, 0.08); border: 1px solid rgba(46, 213, 115, 0.25); border-left: 3px solid #2ed573; padding: 12px 16px; border-radius: 6px; margin-bottom: 20px; font-size: 13px; color: #cbd5e1;\"><strong style=\"color: #2ed573;\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\" style=\"margin-right:6px;\"></i>Beginner Tip:</strong> Use recursion (DFS) to explore paths deeply, or a queue (BFS) to process nodes level by level.</div><p>Given the <code>root</code> of an n-ary tree, return <em>the preorder traversal of its nodes&#39; values</em>.</p>\n\n<p>Nary-Tree input serialization is represented in their level order traversal. Each group of children is separated by the null value (See examples)</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<div style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; color: #38bdf8; margin-top: 20px; margin-bottom: 8px;\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\" style=\"margin-right:6px;\"></i>Example 1:</div>\n\n<p><img src=\"https://assets.leetcode.com/uploads/2018/10/12/narytreeexample.png\" style=\"width: 100%; max-width: 300px;\" /></p>\n\n<div style=\"background: #090d16; border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.08); border-left: 3px solid #38bdf8; border-radius: 6px; padding: 12px 16px; font-family: 'SFMono-Regular', Consolas, monospace; font-size: 13px; line-height: 1.6; margin: 6px 0 16px 0; color: #e2e8f0;\"><div style=\"margin-bottom: 6px;\"><span style=\"color: #94a3b8; font-weight: 600;\">Input:</span> <code style=\"color: #38bdf8; background: rgba(56, 189, 248, 0.08); padding: 2px 6px; border-radius: 4px;\">root = [1,null,3,2,4,null,5,6]</code></div><div><span style=\"color: #94a3b8; font-weight: 600;\">Output:</span> <code style=\"color: #2ed573; background: rgba(46, 213, 115, 0.08); padding: 2px 6px; border-radius: 4px;\">[1,3,5,6,2,4]</code></div></div>\n\n<div style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; color: #38bdf8; margin-top: 20px; margin-bottom: 8px;\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\" style=\"margin-right:6px;\"></i>Example 2:</div>\n\n<p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://assets.leetcode.com/uploads/2019/11/08/sample_4_964.png\" style=\"width: 296px; height: 241px;\" /></p>\n\n<div style=\"background: #090d16; border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.08); border-left: 3px solid #38bdf8; border-radius: 6px; padding: 12px 16px; font-family: 'SFMono-Regular', Consolas, monospace; font-size: 13px; line-height: 1.6; margin: 6px 0 16px 0; color: #e2e8f0;\"><div style=\"margin-bottom: 6px;\"><span style=\"color: #94a3b8; font-weight: 600;\">Input:</span> <code style=\"color: #38bdf8; background: rgba(56, 189, 248, 0.08); padding: 2px 6px; border-radius: 4px;\">root = [1,null,2,3,4,5,null,null,6,7,null,8,null,9,10,null,null,11,null,12,null,13,null,null,14]</code></div><div><span style=\"color: #94a3b8; font-weight: 600;\">Output:</span> <code style=\"color: #2ed573; background: rgba(46, 213, 115, 0.08); padding: 2px 6px; border-radius: 4px;\">[1,2,3,6,7,11,14,4,8,12,5,9,13,10]</code></div></div>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<div style=\"font-weight: 700; font-size: 14px; color: #ffc01e; margin-top: 24px; margin-bottom: 10px;\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\" style=\"margin-right:6px;\"></i>Constraints:</div>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li>The number of nodes in the tree is in the range <code>[0, 10<sup>4</sup>]</code>.</li>\n\t<li><code>0 &lt;= Node.val &lt;= 10<sup>4</sup></code></li>\n\t<li>The height of the n-ary tree is less than or equal to <code>1000</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><div style=\"font-weight: 700; font-size: 14px; color: #a855f7; margin-top: 24px; margin-bottom: 10px;\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-rocket\" style=\"margin-right:6px;\"></i>Follow-up Challenge:</div> Recursive solution is trivial, could you do it iteratively?</p>",
        "companies": [
            "Amazon",
            "Google"
        ],
        "tags": [
            "Stack",
            "Tree",
            "Depth-First Search"
        ],
        "constraints": [
            "The number of nodes in the tree is in the range [0, 104].",
            "0 <= Node.val <= 104",
            "The height of the n-ary tree is less than or equal to 1000."
        ],
        "hints": [
            "Decide between Depth-First Search (DFS) for branch recursion or Breadth-First Search (BFS) for shortest path/level order.",
            "Maintain a visited structure (Hash Set or boolean array) for undirected graphs to prevent infinite cycles.",
            "Analyze recursion stack depth: in the worst case of a skewed tree, DFS recursion stack space can be O(N)."
        ],
