        "title": "Second Minimum Node In a Binary Tree",
        "category": "Trees & Graphs",
        "difficulty": "Easy",
        "description": "<div style=\"background: rgba(46, 213, 115, 0.08); border: 1px solid rgba(46, 213, 115, 0.25); border-left: 3px solid #2ed573; padding: 12px 16px; border-radius: 6px; margin-bottom: 20px; font-size: 13px; color: #cbd5e1;\"><strong style=\"color: #2ed573;\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\" style=\"margin-right:6px;\"></i>Beginner Tip:</strong> Use recursion (DFS) to explore paths deeply, or a queue (BFS) to process nodes level by level.</div><p>Given a non-empty special binary tree consisting of nodes with the non-negative value, where each node in this tree has exactly <code>two</code> or <code>zero</code> sub-node. If the node has two sub-nodes, then this node&#39;s value is the smaller value among its two sub-nodes. More formally, the property&nbsp;<code>root.val = min(root.left.val, root.right.val)</code>&nbsp;always holds.</p>\n\n<p>Given such a binary tree, you need to output the <b>second minimum</b> value in the set made of all the nodes&#39; value in the whole tree.</p>\n\n<p>If no such second minimum value exists, output -1 instead.</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<div style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; color: #38bdf8; margin-top: 20px; margin-bottom: 8px;\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\" style=\"margin-right:6px;\"></i>Example 1:</div>\n<img alt=\"\" src=\"https://assets.leetcode.com/uploads/2020/10/15/smbt1.jpg\" style=\"width: 431px; height: 302px;\" />\n<div style=\"background: #090d16; border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.08); border-left: 3px solid #38bdf8; border-radius: 6px; padding: 12px 16px; font-family: 'SFMono-Regular', Consolas, monospace; font-size: 13px; line-height: 1.6; margin: 6px 0 16px 0; color: #e2e8f0;\"><div style=\"margin-bottom: 6px;\"><span style=\"color: #94a3b8; font-weight: 600;\">Input:</span> <code style=\"color: #38bdf8; background: rgba(56, 189, 248, 0.08); padding: 2px 6px; border-radius: 4px;\">root = [2,2,5,null,null,5,7]</code></div><div><span style=\"color: #94a3b8; font-weight: 600;\">Output:</span> <code style=\"color: #2ed573; background: rgba(46, 213, 115, 0.08); padding: 2px 6px; border-radius: 4px;\">5</code></div><div style=\"color: #a1a1aa; font-size: 12px; font-family: sans-serif; margin-top: 8px; padding-top: 8px; border-top: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.06);\"><b>Explanation:</b> The smallest value is 2, the second smallest value is 5.</div></div>\n\n<div style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; color: #38bdf8; margin-top: 20px; margin-bottom: 8px;\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\" style=\"margin-right:6px;\"></i>Example 2:</div>\n<img alt=\"\" src=\"https://assets.leetcode.com/uploads/2020/10/15/smbt2.jpg\" style=\"width: 321px; height: 182px;\" />\n<div style=\"background: #090d16; border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.08); border-left: 3px solid #38bdf8; border-radius: 6px; padding: 12px 16px; font-family: 'SFMono-Regular', Consolas, monospace; font-size: 13px; line-height: 1.6; margin: 6px 0 16px 0; color: #e2e8f0;\"><div style=\"margin-bottom: 6px;\"><span style=\"color: #94a3b8; font-weight: 600;\">Input:</span> <code style=\"color: #38bdf8; background: rgba(56, 189, 248, 0.08); padding: 2px 6px; border-radius: 4px;\">root = [2,2,2]</code></div><div><span style=\"color: #94a3b8; font-weight: 600;\">Output:</span> <code style=\"color: #2ed573; background: rgba(46, 213, 115, 0.08); padding: 2px 6px; border-radius: 4px;\">-1</code></div><div style=\"color: #a1a1aa; font-size: 12px; font-family: sans-serif; margin-top: 8px; padding-top: 8px; border-top: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.06);\"><b>Explanation:</b> The smallest value is 2, but there isn&#39;t any second smallest value.</div></div>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<div style=\"font-weight: 700; font-size: 14px; color: #ffc01e; margin-top: 24px; margin-bottom: 10px;\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\" style=\"margin-right:6px;\"></i>Constraints:</div>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li>The number of nodes in the tree is in the range <code>[1, 25]</code>.</li>\n\t<li><code>1 &lt;= Node.val &lt;= 2<sup>31</sup> - 1</code></li>\n\t<li><code>root.val == min(root.left.val, root.right.val)</code>&nbsp;for each internal node of the tree.</li>\n</ul>",
        "companies": [
            "LinkedIn",
            "Meta"
        ],
        "tags": [
            "Tree",
            "Depth-First Search",
            "Binary Tree"
        ],
        "constraints": [
            "The number of nodes in the tree is in the range [1, 25].",
            "1 <= Node.val <= 231 - 1",
            "root.val == min(root.left.val, root.right.val) for each internal node of the tree."
        ],
        "hints": [
            "Decide between Depth-First Search (DFS) for branch recursion or Breadth-First Search (BFS) for shortest path/level order.",
            "Maintain a visited structure (Hash Set or boolean array) for undirected graphs to prevent infinite cycles.",
            "Analyze recursion stack depth: in the worst case of a skewed tree, DFS recursion stack space can be O(N)."
        ],
